  "query_preview": "..Main concerns today from the isolated to scattered\nshowers and thunderstorms that develop this afternoon are 1) how\nfar west will the convection be, 2) isolated severe wind gusts,\n3) blowing dust and 4) brief moderate rain in this pre-season \ntaste of the upcoming monsoon.\n\nThe combination of increasing low-level easterly pressure \ngradient and thunderstorm outflows from NM has pushed 40+ \nsurface dewpoints into the eastern areas this morning with \nthese high dewpoints continuing to push past Tucson later this \nmorning before mixing out this afternoon. PWAT values are also \nincreasing with the latest GOES imagery showing values between \n0.60\" and 0.85\" east of Tucson. Now on to the number of concerns\nfor today. \n\n1) Models are still in a quandary on how far west convection \nwill be later today with NBM/ensembles/deterministics keeping \nstorms across Graham, Greenlee and Cochise counties while CAMs \nhave storms across eastern portions of Pinal, Pima and Santa \nCruz counties, which coincides with the western extent of SPCs \ngeneral thunderstorm outlook area for today. I sided with the \nCAMs/SPC which meant that I had to increase PoPs for the western\nextent area versus what the baseline NBM was showing. Low end \nprobability that a few storms may develop as far west as central\nPima county as a weak convergence area may develop between the \nmostly washed out outflow boundary and sea-breeze that pushes \ninto central Pima county from the GLFofCA. \n\n2) with the low-levels mostly on the dry side producing \ninverted-V sounding profiles and forecast DCAPE values between \n1600-1800 j/kg, some of the thunderstorms may produce isolated \nwind gusts between 50-60 mph, especially east of Tucson where \n03/00z HREF has a small 10% area of 50+ knots near Safford with \nstorms pushing off the Rim. \n\n3) the gusty outflow winds will be capable of producing areas \nof blowing dust. \n\n4) although the low-levels are fairly dry, some of the\nthunderstorms east of Tucson may produce rainfall amounts\nbetween 0.25\" and 0.50\".\n\nWell that's a lot to write for one day.\n\nThursday: Moisture lingers for a slight chance of showers and\nthunderstorms mainly east of Tucson. Near normal highs.\n\nFriday thru Tuesday: Dry with near or slightly above normal high\ntemperatures with the area between trofiness to the W/NW and\nhigh pressure to the E/SE.\n\n8-14 day outlooks (June 10-16)\nTemperature: Likely above normal (54%-57% chance)\nPrecipitation: Near normal (36% chance)",
